# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: dxf-angle-units-hatch-viewport-tables — HATCH / VIEWPORT / table-record angles still copied unconverted from DXF degrees
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: dxf-file-angle-units
#+BRANCH: fix-dxf-angles-hatch-viewport
#+DATE: 2026-10-22

* Symptom

After dxf-file-angle-units, the DXF codec still copies unconverted the
degree angles of HATCH 52 (pattern angle) / 53 (pattern line angles) and
the HATCH boundary path's ARC and ELLIPSE edge 50/51 start/end angles
(nested in the boundary data, so the "first occurrence per code" rule of
=*DXF-DEGREE-GROUPS*= in =clautolisp/drawing/source/dxf.lisp= does not fit
them), VIEWPORT 51 (twist), table records VPORT 50/51, VIEW 50, and header
angle variables. Check each against the DXF reference before converting.

* Fix (2026-10-22)

Reference basis: the DXF reference's group-code table, "50-58: Angles
(output in degrees to DXF files and radians through AutoLISP and ObjectARX
applications)", and the per-object group lists. cador's HATCH command
(=cador/source/command-hatch.lisp=) already stores 52, 53 and the arc-edge
50/51 in radians, as measured on BricsCAD (round 2, job 16916238723) and
AutoCAD (arc edge 51 = 2pi), so the codec now agrees with it.

=clautolisp/drawing/source/dxf.lisp=:
- new =*dxf-repeated-degree-groups*= = (("HATCH" 50 51 52 53)): for such a
  type EVERY occurrence is converted, up to a 101 / 1001 stop
  (=*dxf-repeated-degree-scan-stop-codes*=). Within a HATCH these codes are
  used only for angles -- 52 pattern angle, 53 once per pattern line (after
  78), 50/51 of each circular-arc (72 = 2) and elliptic-arc (72 = 3)
  boundary edge; the polyline-path 72/73 flags, the spline-edge groups and
  the gradient 450-470 groups (460 is radians in the file) use other codes.
  This replaces a structured walk of the boundary data, which would give
  the same result with more ways to go wrong (polyline vs edge paths,
  islands, spline edges). HATCH's own 75/76 come before 52/53, which is why
  it cannot use the 75/76 stop rule of =*dxf-degree-groups*=.
- =*dxf-degree-groups*= gains VIEWPORT 50/51 (snap angle, view twist) and
  the table records VPORT 50/51 (snap rotation, view twist), VIEW 50
  (twist) and STYLE 50 (oblique angle; not in the ticket, same class).
- =dxf-load-tables= converts each record (degrees -> radians) and
  =dxf-write-tables= writes a converted copy (radians -> degrees): table
  records are AutoLISP-visible as stored, through =tblsearch= / =tblnext=
  (=cador/source/table-api.lisp=). No cador code creates VPORT / VIEW /
  STYLE records with angles (VPORTS Save writes 10/11 only), so nothing
  else stored degrees.

Elliptic-arc edges: the reference lists their 50/51 as "start angle" /
"end angle", in the 50-58 angle range, and ezdxf reads them as degrees;
whether the value is a true angle or an ellipse parameter does not matter
for the unit conversion. Not probed on a vendor (cador never builds them).

Left out:
- header variables ($ANGBASE, $SNAPANG, ...): =dxf-load-header= stores
  them as drawing variables, but cador's =getvar= never reads those (its
  sysvars come from the catalogue), so they are not AutoLISP-visible; the
  header is written back as read, consistently. Their file units are also
  not settled by the reference text.
- other 50-58 groups nobody asked for (LIGHT 50/51 hotspot / falloff,
  MLINESTYLE 51/52 in OBJECTS): follow-up if needed.

Tests (=clautolisp/drawing/tests/dxf-tests.lisp=):
- =dxf-read-converts-hatch-and-viewport-angles= -- a HATCH with pattern
  angle 30, two pattern lines 75 / 165, a circular-arc edge 0..90 and an
  elliptic-arc edge 90..180 reads as radians; radius 40, scale 41, dashes
  49 untouched; VIEWPORT 50/51 = 10/20 read as pi/18, pi/9;
- =dxf-read-converts-table-record-angles= -- VPORT 50/51, VIEW 50, STYLE 50;
- =dxf-write-emits-hatch-and-viewport-degrees= -- a cador-shaped HATCH
  (radians) writes 52 = 30, 53 = 75, arc edge 51 = 90; VIEWPORT 51 = 20;
- =dxf-hatch-viewport-table-angle-round-trip= -- read, add a cador HATCH,
  write, read: all angles come back; the source is not mutated.

Red before the codec change (=make test-sbcl
TEST_SYSTEM=clautolisp/drawing/tests=): 383 checks, 22 failures ("HATCH 52 =
30 deg must read as pi/6, got 30.0d0", "arc edge end 90 deg must read as
pi/2, got 90.0d0", "VPORT 50 = 15 deg must read as pi/12, got 15.0d0",
"VIEWPORT 51 in the file must be 20: 0.349...", ...). Green after: 383
checks, 0 failures; =clautolisp/cador/tests= 1897 checks, 0 failures; full
=make test-sbcl= Fail 0 in every suite.

Status NEXT, not DONE: no vendor-written DXF / tblsearch probe of a HATCH
with an elliptic edge, a VIEWPORT, or VPORT / VIEW / STYLE records yet.

* Probe (2026-10-23)

Covered by the same probe and fixture test as dxf-file-angle-units (see
its "Probe (2026-10-23)" section): =dxf-angles-probe.lsp=,
=scripts/run-dxf-angles-probe.sh= / =.ps1=, jobs =dxf-angles:probe:*=, and
=dxf-vendor-fixtures-angle-units= over
=clautolisp/drawing/tests/fixtures/vendor-dxf/=. Its cases for this
ticket:

- three HATCHes ANSI31 at pattern angle 30, made with
  =-HATCH _P ANSI31 1 30 _S <objects>= (entmakex fallback when the command
  gives no HATCH, recorded as =DXFANG CASE ... entmake=): a CIRCLE boundary
  (circular-arc edge 0..360), an ELLIPSE (elliptic-arc edge -- the least
  certain group), an ARC 30..150 closed by a LINE; printed: 52, every 53,
  72 and the edge 50/51, in entget order and in file order;
- SNAPANG 20 degrees -> the VPORT *Active record's 50 (tblobjname +
  entget, and the file's VPORT record);
- a STYLE record DXFANG with oblique 10 and a VIEW record DXFANG with
  twist 45, both by entmake.

VIEWPORT 50/51 is not probed (paper space). Close with the other ticket.
Baseline (clautolisp): the CIRCLE hatch comes from cador's -HATCH, the
ELLIPSE and ARC+LINE ones fall back to entmakex (cador's -HATCH takes no
such boundary), all consistent (file = entget * 180/pi); the STYLE /
VIEW entmakes land in ENTITIES (entmake-symbol-table-records.issue); SNAPANG
does not reach the VPORT *Active record, whose entget also lacks the 5
handle (vport-active-snapang-and-handle.issue).
